{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## What was wrong\n\n`is_expired` in `repo/cache.py` documented that an entry expires when `age >= ttl`, but the implementation used `age > ttl`. At exactly the TTL (e.g. 60 seconds old with a 60s TTL), entries were still treated as fresh for one extra tick. That showed up in summaries via `cache_summary` in `repo/rows.py`, which delegates to `is_expired`.\n\n## Fix\n\nUse inclusive comparison so the boundary matches the docstring:\n\n```python\nreturn age >= ttl\n```\n\nRemoved the stale BUG comment that described the old behavior.\n\n`old_ttl_check` already forwards to `is_expired`, so v1 callers pick up the fix without changes. All tests in `tests/visible/test_cache.py` pass."}]},"session_id":"10e3ae09-c20b-4605-993e-1b9849d3ddc0"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":35272,"duration_api_ms":35272,"is_error":false,"result":"Investigating cache expiry logic in `repo/cache.py` and related helpers.\n\n\nFixing boundary condition: docstring says `age == ttl` is expired, but code used `age > ttl`.\n## What was wrong\n\n`is_expired` in `repo/cache.py` documented that an entry expires when `age >= ttl`, but the implementation used `age > ttl`. At exactly the TTL (e.g. 60 seconds old with a 60s TTL), entries were still treated as fresh for one extra tick. That showed up in summaries via `cache_summary` in `repo/rows.py`, which delegates to `is_expired`.\n\n## Fix\n\nUse inclusive comparison so the boundary matches the docstring:\n\n```python\nreturn age >= ttl\n```\n\nRemoved the stale BUG comment that described the old behavior.\n\n`old_ttl_check` already forwards to `is_expired`, so v1 callers pick up the fix without changes. All tests in `tests/visible/test_cache.py` pass.","session_id":"10e3ae09-c20b-4605-993e-1b9849d3ddc0","request_id":"e3ea7ecb-1e2b-4c4c-b497-c9218ce8824e","usage":{"inputTokens":12229,"outputTokens":1293,"cacheReadTokens":92998,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
